<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Shapes on Our Plates – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes on Our Plates</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's a Square Cracker.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Use pictures or objects to express ideas. Concept: Use facial expressions and simple gestures to help explain feelings or needs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use gestures or body language to describe food items (e.g., pretending to hold a round apple). Can clarify responses using pictures or pointing to food-related visuals (e.g., showing the picture of a cracker to clarify its meaning). Can use facial expressions and gestures to help describe food preferences and shapes (e.g., pretending to take a bite of an apple and using the hands to show that it is round).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and starts with a quick "Shape and Food Chant." The teacher shows flashcards of different food items (apple, cracker, banana) and shapes (round, square, oval). Students clap and repeat the names and shapes, using simple gestures. For example, the teacher holds a "round" apple flashcard and makes a circle with hands, saying "Round apple!" and students imitate.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to use gestures and simple words to explain food shapes. The teacher holds up a picture of a cracker and says, "This is a cracker. It is square." while making a square shape with their hands. Then, the teacher holds up a picture of a banana and says, "This is a banana. It is oval." while making an oval shape. The teacher emphasizes the /k/ sound in "cracker" and /r/ in "round."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by asking simple questions and observing responses.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a round apple picture) "Is this a square apple?" (Students shake head or say "No.") "What shape is it?" (Students say "Round!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a cracker picture) "What is this?" (Students say "Cracker!") "Is it round?" (Students shake head or say "No.") "What shape is the cracker?" (Students make square gesture or say "Square!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shapes on Our Plates".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large flashcards or a poster featuring different food items (apple, orange, banana, pineapple, lemon, lime, mango, papaya, cracker) clearly displaying their shapes. Each food item is labeled with its name and shape (e.g., "Round apple," "Square cracker"). The register is simple and informative, suitable for 1st graders.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying food shapes by using simple words and pointing, and engaging in simple questions about food items, such as "What shape is the apple?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Point and Say:</b> The teacher points to a food item on the poster (e.g., a lemon) and asks, "What is this?" (Students say "Lemon!"). Then, the teacher asks, "What shape is it?" (Students respond with "Oval!" or point to the oval shape word).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Show Me" Game:</b> The teacher calls out a food item and its shape, e.g., "Show me the round orange!" Students point to the correct picture on the poster. The teacher then asks, "Is it big or small?" and students use gestures (big hands, small hands).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Match the Shape:</b> The teacher holds up a shape cutout (e.g., a square) and asks students to point to a food on the poster that matches that shape (e.g., "Cracker!").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using gestures and simple words (Adjective + noun: "round apple," "square cracker"; WH-questions: "What shape is it?"; Present simple: "Oranges are round.") to describe and clarify food items and their shapes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Gesture-Say-Match (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher shows a picture of a food item (e.g., banana). Students are encouraged to use a gesture (e.g., a curved motion for oval) and say, "This is a banana. It is oval." The teacher repeats and guides, ensuring students accurately pair gestures with the food and shape description. They practice saying /r/ in "round" and /k/ in "cracker."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Explain My Food" (Semi-controlled):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of food flashcards. Student A picks a card (e.g., a mango) but does not show it. Student A uses gestures (e.g., an oval shape with hands) and simple words (e.g., "It is a fruit. It is orange.") to describe it. Student B guesses the food and asks, "What shape is it?" Student A then says, "It is oval." They take turns.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Clarify the Shape" (Pair Interaction):</b> Students in pairs look at their "Food Shape Match" project visuals (pictures of foods). Student A points to a food item (e.g., a lemon) and says, "This is a lemon." Student B asks, "What shape is it?" If Student A struggles to articulate, they are encouraged to use gestures (e.g., making an oval shape with hands) or point to a shape chart to clarify, then try to say "It is oval."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shapes on Our Plates".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative presentation of the "Food Shape Match" project posters. Students will present their collected food pictures and match them to shapes, using gestures and simple descriptions to mediate the information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups, each with their "Food Shape Match" poster (pictures of foods matched to shapes like round oranges, square crackers).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group takes turns presenting their poster to the class or to another group.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student points to a food item on their poster (e.g., a cracker picture) and says, "This is a cracker." They use their hands to make a square shape and say, "It is square."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Another student points to a different food (e.g., an apple) and says, "This is an apple." They make a round gesture and say, "It is round."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to use facial expressions (e.g., "tasty" smile) and gestures (e.g., pretending to eat) when describing food preferences or qualities. If a student struggles with a word, they are prompted to point to the picture or use a gesture to clarify.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on mediating information about the food shapes using both verbal (simple words) and non-verbal (gestures, pointing) communication, reflecting the project's goal.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Food Shape Match" project presentations. The teacher circulates, observing each student's ability to describe and clarify using mediation strategies.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present two food items from their "Food Shape Match" poster. For each item, they must: 1) name the food, 2) describe its shape using a simple sentence (e.g., "It is a round orange."), and 3) use a gesture or body language to demonstrate the shape or a preference (e.g., making a round shape with hands, pretending to eat the food with a happy face).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Gestures/Body Language:</b> Effectively uses gestures (e.g., for round, square) or facial expressions to describe food or preferences.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity through Pointing/Pictures:</b> Clearly points to pictures on the poster to clarify meaning.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Vocabulary:</b> Uses at least 3-4 target food and shape vocabulary words (e.g., apple, cracker, round, square).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Pronounces key words (e.g., /r/ in "round," /k/ in "cracker") clearly enough to be understood.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participates actively and helps group members during the presentation.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher asks students to show thumbs up/down for "I can use my hands to show 'round'" and "I can show 'square' with my hands." Then, "Did you use pictures to help explain your food shapes?" (Students point to YES or NO visuals).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompts:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was your favorite food shape to explain today? How did you explain it (with words, hands, or pointing)?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new food or shape did you learn to show with your hands or words?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? The students were very engaged with the project and using gestures to explain. Their ability to point to pictures and combine simple words with body language was good for Pre-A1.3.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next time we focus on mediation with young learners? Some students needed more prompting to use full simple sentences ("It is round"). Providing sentence frames on the board for "This is a _______. It is _______." could further support verbal output while maintaining the mediation focus with gestures. Perhaps integrate more "show me happy/sad face for food" to practice facial expression mediation.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Shapes on Our Plates" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, likely beginning with listening and vocabulary related to a new topic, building on the multi-sensory and communicative approaches used in this unit.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>